1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,319
Do you know the difference between saying
"Luis sets the table" and "Luis...

2
00:00:06,319 --> 00:00:14,240
turns me on"? Today I'll show you six
colloquial expressions with the verb PONER.

3
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:20,600
First expression: "poner la mesa" —
it means to set the table.

4
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:26,160
Second expression: "ponerse de los
nervios". We use it

5
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:30,279
to say something or someone is
getting on our nerves.

6
00:00:30,279 --> 00:00:35,239
Third expression: "ponerte entre la
espada y la pared" —

7
00:00:35,239 --> 00:00:41,000
when something or someone forces you
to choose, with no way out.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Fourth expression: "ponerse las pilas" —
meaning, to really get your act together.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:56,199
Fifth expression: "ponerse las botas".
We say this when we've eaten a ton and

10
00:00:56,199 --> 00:01:00,920
loved every bite. And the sixth
expression,

11
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:07,400
"Luis me pone" — in colloquial speech,
it means someone turns you on

12
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:12,080
physically.
Take care.
